{%- comment -%} mode: milk | nofilter | north | vietnam | moka | black | pourover | frenchpress | turkish | coldbrew | kattan | chukku | grind {%- endcomment -%}
<ol class="cf__steps">
  {%- case mode -%}
    {%- when 'milk' -%}
      <li><span>Put <b>2 to 3 level tablespoons (about 15 to 20 g)</b> of filter coffee powder in the top chamber of the filter and level it.</span></li>
      <li><span>Set the press disc (the "umbrella") on top and <b>press lightly</b>. Packed hard, the coffee barely drips.</span></li>
      <li><span>Boil water, let it rest about 30 seconds (<b>90 to 95 °C</b>), then pour slowly until the top chamber is about three-quarters full. Close the lid.</span></li>
      <li><span>Leave it to drip for <b>10 to 20 minutes</b> without stirring. This first decoction is the strongest; pouring water again gives a lighter second one.</span></li>
      <li><span>Boil full-fat milk until it rises.</span></li>
      <li><span>In the tumbler: sugar if you like, then <b>1 part decoction to 3 or 4 parts hot milk</b>, about 2 to 4 tablespoons of decoction per cup.</span></li>
      <li><span>Pour between the tumbler and the davara 3 or 4 times, from a height, for the froth. Poured from arm's height, this is the "meter kaapi" of coffee houses.</span></li>
    {%- when 'nofilter' -%}
      <li><span>Boil water, take the pan off the heat and wait about 30 seconds.</span></li>
      <li><span>Stir in <b>1 level tablespoon of powder for every 60 ml</b> of water.</span></li>
      <li><span>Cover and leave for <b>5 minutes</b>.</span></li>
      <li><span>Strain twice through a fine cloth or a fine tea strainer.</span></li>
      <li><span>Use it like filter decoction: 1 part to 3 or 4 parts hot milk.</span></li>
    {%- when 'north' -%}
      <li><span>In a small pan, boil <b>¼ cup of water with 1½ to 2 teaspoons</b> of filter coffee powder for 1 to 2 minutes.</span></li>
      <li><span>Add <b>1 cup of milk</b> and sugar to taste. Bring it to a boil, stirring.</span></li>
      <li><span>Simmer for 1 minute, then strain through a fine tea strainer into the cup.</span></li>
      <li><span>For froth, pour between the pan and the cup a few times, or whisk.</span></li>
    {%- when 'vietnam' -%}
      <li><span>Put <b>1 to 2 tablespoons of sweetened condensed milk</b> in a glass.</span></li>
      <li><span>Add <b>10 to 14 g</b> (about 2 heaped tablespoons) of coffee to a Vietnamese phin, or to the top chamber of a South Indian filter. Press lightly.</span></li>
      <li><span>Pour a splash of water just off the boil to wet the coffee, wait 30 seconds, then fill up.</span></li>
      <li><span>Let it drip onto the condensed milk for about <b>4 to 5 minutes</b>.</span></li>
      <li><span>Stir well. Drink it hot, or pour it over ice for <i>cà phê sữa đá</i>.</span></li>
    {%- when 'moka' -%}
      <li><span>Fill the bottom chamber with hot water <b>up to the valve</b>.</span></li>
      <li><span>Fill the basket with coffee and level it. <b>Do not press it down.</b></span></li>
      <li><span>Screw the pot together and heat on medium-low with the lid open.</span></li>
      <li><span>When the coffee turns pale and starts to gurgle, take it off the heat.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Latte:</b> 1 part moka coffee to 3 parts hot frothed milk. <b>Black:</b> top up with hot water to taste.</span></li>
    {%- when 'black' -%}
      <li><span>Put <b>2 level tablespoons</b> of pure coffee powder in the top chamber of a steel filter, press lightly and pour water just off the boil to three-quarters full.</span></li>
      <li><span>When it has dripped, pour the decoction into a mug and <b>top up with hot water</b>, about 1 part decoction to 2 or 3 parts water.</span></li>
      <li><span>Drink it as it is, or with a little sugar, jaggery or palm jaggery (karupatti).</span></li>
    {%- when 'pourover' -%}
      <li><span>Use <b>15 g of coffee for 240 ml of water</b> (1 : 16), medium grind. Rinse the paper filter with hot water and discard that water.</span></li>
      <li><span>Heat water to <b>90 to 96 °C</b>, about 30 seconds off the boil.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Bloom:</b> pour about 30 ml, twice the weight of the coffee, and wait 30 to 45 seconds while it puffs up.</span></li>
      <li><span>Pour the rest slowly in small circles. The whole brew should take <b>3 to 4 minutes</b>.</span></li>
    {%- when 'frenchpress' -%}
      <li><span>Grind <b>coarse</b>, like rough salt: <b>20 g of coffee for 300 ml</b> of water (about 1 : 15).</span></li>
      <li><span>Pour water just off the boil over the coffee and stir once.</span></li>
      <li><span>Put the lid on and steep for <b>4 minutes</b>.</span></li>
      <li><span>Press slowly, then pour it all out so it does not keep brewing and turn bitter.</span></li>
    {%- when 'turkish' -%}
      <li><span>Grind beans to a <b>fine powder</b>, finer than filter coffee.</span></li>
      <li><span>For each cup, put <b>1 heaped teaspoon (about 7 g)</b> of coffee and <b>60 to 70 ml of cold water</b> in a cezve or small pan. Add sugar now if you take it, and stir.</span></li>
      <li><span>Heat slowly on low. <b>Do not stir again.</b></span></li>
      <li><span>When the foam rises, after about 3 minutes, take it off just before it boils over.</span></li>
      <li><span>Spoon the foam into the cup, pour the coffee, and let the grounds settle for a minute before sipping.</span></li>
    {%- when 'coldbrew' -%}
      <li><span>Grind <b>coarse</b>: <b>100 g of coffee to 800 ml of cold water</b> (1 : 8).</span></li>
      <li><span>Stir, cover and keep in the fridge for <b>12 to 16 hours</b>.</span></li>
      <li><span>Strain through a fine cloth or a paper filter.</span></li>
      <li><span>Mix 1 part of this concentrate with 1 part water or milk, over ice.</span></li>
    {%- when 'kattan' -%}
      <li><span>Boil <b>1 cup of water</b> with jaggery to taste until it dissolves.</span></li>
      <li><span>Take it off the heat, stir in <b>1 teaspoon of pure coffee powder</b> and cover for 2 to 3 minutes.</span></li>
      <li><span>Strain into a glass. Kattan kaapi is meant to be light and amber, not thick. Many homes add a pinch of cumin.</span></li>
    {%- when 'chukku' -%}
      <li><span>Boil <b>2 cups of water</b>, add jaggery and stir until it dissolves.</span></li>
      <li><span>Add about <b>½ teaspoon of dry ginger powder</b>, a pinch of cumin, a few crushed <a href="/products/kerala-black-pepper">black peppercorns</a> and <b>1 to 2 teaspoons of coffee powder</b>.</span></li>
      <li><span>Boil for about 4 minutes. Add a few tulsi leaves for the last 2 minutes if you have them.</span></li>
      <li><span>Strain and drink hot. It is a traditional Kerala monsoon drink, best as an occasional cup.</span></li>
    {%- when 'grind' -%}
      <li><span>Grind only what you need, <b>just before brewing</b>. Ground coffee loses aroma within days.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Turkish:</b> powder-fine. <b>Steel filter:</b> fine, close to table salt. <b>Moka pot:</b> fine to medium.</span></li>
      <li><span><b>Pour-over or drip:</b> medium, like coarse sand. <b>French press and cold brew:</b> coarse.</span></li>
      <li><span>No grinder? A dry mixer jar works: grind in <b>short pulses of 3 to 4 seconds</b>, resting in between so the beans do not heat up.</span></li>
  {%- endcase -%}
</ol>
